==Old English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{ang-IPA|trēow}}

===Etymology 1===
{{etymon|ang|id=tree|inh|gmw-pro>*treu>tree}}
From {{inh|ang|gmw-pro|*treu}}, from {{inh|ang|gem-pro|*trewą}}. Germanic cognates include {{cog|ofs|trē}}, {{cog|osx|trio}}, {{cog|non|tré}}, {{cog|got|𐍄𐍂𐌹𐌿}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{alter|ang|trēo|trīo|trīow|trȳw|trēu|trēw}}

====Noun====
{{ang-noun|n|head=trēow}}

# {{topic|ang|Trees}}[[tree]]
#* [https://archive.org/details/gospelaccording00skeagoog/page/n194/mode/2up "Gospel of Saint Luke"], chapter 19, verse 4
#*: {{quote|ang|''Þā arn hē beforan and stāh ūp on ān '''trēow'''. Sicomorum þ hē hine ġesāwe. forþām þe hē wolde þanon faran...''|Then he ran in front and climbed (lit. ascended) up on a '''tree'''. From sycamores that he saw him. For which he would go from there.}}
# {{topic|ang|Woods}}[[wood]]
# {{topic|ang|Forests}}(collective) trees, a [[forest]]
# a piece of [[wood]]: [[beam]], [[log]], [[stake]], [[staff]], [[cudgel]]
#* '''late 9th century''', [https://archive.org/details/kingalfredsorosi79oros/page/158/mode/2up translation] of [[w:Orosius|Orosius’]] ''History Against the Pagans''
#*: {{quote|ang|''⁊ hæfdon ġeleornad mā cræfta hū hīe þā elpendas beswīcan mehton, mid þǣm þæt hīe nāmon '''trēowu''', ⁊ slōgon on oþerne ende moniġe sċearpe īsene næġlas, ⁊ hīe mid flexe bewundon, ⁊ onbǣrndon hit, ⁊ beþyddan hit þonne on þone elpend hindan...''|And they had learned more tricks through which they could ensnare the elephants; they took '''stakes''' and drove into the other end many sharp iron nails, and wrapped them in flax, and set them on fire, and then thrust them into the elephant from behind...}}
# [[gallows]]; [[cross]]

